  "summary": "The change is well-scoped, well-commented, and thoroughly tested: it correctly routes start/stop through launchd/systemd, adds a live 'Supervised:' reading that names the DETACHED/FAILING states (gt-sq9e), and the tests are hermetic (HOME/XDG_DATA_HOME isolation, no host-state leakage). The core design — bootout/stop through the supervisor, bootstrap on start after a bootout — is sound and fail-closed on the start path. The blocking issue is that the stop path is fail-open: when the supervisor job's live state cannot be read, or when the supervisor stop command itself fails, the code falls back to a direct signal and reports 'Daemon stopped' — the exact failure mode the change exists to prevent, because a signal alone leaves the loaded KeepAlive job to respawn the daemon. There are also two minor secondary issues (a stop-after-bootout race that can spuriously error, and stale/overgeneralized operator-facing stop instructions).",
